        "title": "Historical Cordage: Ancient Egypt, Inca Quipu, Viking Rigging, Sailmaking",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "3500 BCE ── Egypt: papyrus twisted rope, reed rope — chariot and ship rigging\n  2500 BCE ── Egypt: esparto grass rope (Twelfth Dynasty ships: hemp and papyrus)\n  2000 BCE ── China: hemp rope production (Cannabis sativa cultivated for fiber)\n  1000 BCE ── Phoenicia: hemp rigging on Mediterranean sailing vessels\n  400  BCE ── Rome: hemp and flax ropes; pitch-coated for moisture resistance\n  1000 CE  ── Viking Norse: walrus hide + hemp; ropewalks documented\n  1100 CE  ── Medieval Europe: ropewalks established near shipyards\n  1400 CE  ── Inca Empire: quipu system — knotted rope as information storage\n  1400 CE  ── Age of Sail begins: Manila hemp rigging on Spanish galleons\n  1600 CE  ── English ropewalks at Chatham, Portsmouth: Royal Navy supply\n  1800 CE  ── Industrial rope manufacturing: water-powered ropewalk\n  1850 CE  ── Wire rope introduced (Roebling: 1841 wire bridge); replaces some hemp\n  1938 CE  ── DuPont introduces nylon — synthetic rope era begins\n  1943 CE  ── Philippines occupied — US Navy switches from Manila to nylon\n  1970s CE ── Kevlar, Dyneema developed — high-performance era begins\n```"
